ravel
=====

A toy JavaScript/TypeScript runtime written in Rust.

Why
---

To learn how Node, Deno, and Bun actually work.

Those runtimes are mostly not a JavaScript engine. V8 or JavaScriptCore
does the language part. The runtime is everything around it: the event
loop, non-blocking I/O, module resolution, and the web APIs the language
itself does not define. That surrounding machinery is the interesting
part, and the part you cannot see by reading JavaScript.

So ravel embeds an engine and builds that machinery by hand. It is a
learning project. Do not use it for anything real.

What
----

QuickJS runs the JavaScript. Oxc strips TypeScript types and transforms
JSX. Tokio drives timers and file I/O. Everything else is written here:

  Event loop    One loop. Each turn drains microtasks, fires the timer
                that is due, then sleeps until the next deadline or until
                a read completes. It never polls, and it waits on timers
                and I/O together, so neither one holds up the other.

  Async I/O     fs.readFile and fs.writeFile return promises. The read
                happens on Tokio, so the thread is free while the disk is
                busy. Reads issued together run together.

  Modules       Bare imports walk up through node_modules. package.json
                exports and imports maps are honoured, including subpath
                patterns and conditions. A package with an exports map
                publishes only what that map names.

  Sandbox       fs is scoped to the script's directory. Path traversal,
                symlink escape, and null bytes are blocked.

  Errors        Uncaught errors and unhandled rejections print name,
                message, and stack to stderr, then exit 1.

  TypeScript    .ts and .tsx run directly. No build step.

  JSX           .tsx renders to HTML strings, for static sites.

  Web APIs      TextEncoder, TextDecoder, console, process.

Usage
-----

	ravel file.js           run JavaScript
	ravel file.ts           run TypeScript
	ravel                   start the REPL
	ravel --build file.js   run once as a build task
	ravel --serve           serve dist/ over HTTP

	cargo build             build to target/debug/ravel
	cargo install --path .  install `ravel` on your PATH
	cargo test              run the tests

`cargo build` only writes to target/debug/. Run `cargo install --path .`
to update the `ravel` on your PATH.

The examples in examples/ cover each feature and run as-is.

Known limits
------------

tokio::fs is a thread pool, not true async file I/O. Node does the same.
Only io_uring really solves this.

Finding a package is not the same as running it. ravel loads ES modules
only, so a CommonJS package resolves and then fails to load, and a
package reaching for a node global ravel does not have (Buffer, and the
node: builtins) fails at runtime. Pure ESM packages work.

The conditions matched in exports and imports are "ravel", "import",
and "default", in that order. "node" is not one of them: ravel has no
node builtins, so a package's node entry point is the one least likely
to run here, and falling through to "default" picks the portable build.

An extensionless import tries .js, .mjs, .ts, then .tsx, so ./foo finds
foo.js before foo.ts. Node requires the extension in ESM; ravel does
not, which makes ravel accept imports Node would reject.

Delays under 1ms are rounded up to 1ms, as Node does. setTimeout(fn, 0)
does not run fn on the next turn; it runs it a millisecond later.

A top-level await on a promise that nothing can settle exits 0. Once the
loop has no timers and no reads left, there is no work that could settle
it, so ravel stops instead of reporting the stall.

Roadmap
-------

Ordered by what each one teaches, not by usefulness.

  fetch and friends   fetch, Request/Response, URL, crypto,
                      AbortController, streams.

  Multiple isolates   Move the process-global timer state into the
                      engine, so one process can hold several runtimes.
                      Needed for Workers. rquickjs has userdata storage
                      (Ctx::store_userdata) that can hold per-runtime
                      state, which looks like the way in.

